---
import BlogLayout from "../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro";
import { posts } from "../../data/blog";
import { profile } from "../../data/profile";
const post = posts.find(
  (p) => p.slug === "apple-design-award-finalist-wwdc26",
)!;
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<BlogLayout
  title={`${post.title}, Lilly Seay`}
  description={post.description}
  image={post.cover}
>
  <article class="post prose-post">
    <header class="post-head wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Blog · Apple Design Awards 2026 and WWDC26</p>
      <h1>{post.title}</h1>
      <p class="post-meta">
        By {profile.name} · {post.date} · {post.readTime}
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="wrap post-body">
      <figure class="li-embed">
        <iframe
          src="https://www.linkedin.com/embed/feed/update/urn:li:ugcPost:7471272537973252096?compact=1"
          height="399"
          width="504"
          frameborder="0"
          allowfullscreen=""
          title="WWDC26 vlog as an Apple Design Award Finalist, embedded from LinkedIn"
        ></iframe>
        <figcaption>
          My WWDC26 vlog as an Apple Design Award Finalist.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        I have wanted an Apple Design Award for as long as I have been designing
        apps. It is the one award in this industry that is about craft, and for
        ten years it has been the thing I picture when I ask myself what good
        enough looks like. This May, the email came. Hearing Buddy was a 2026
        Apple Design Award Finalist in Inclusivity. This is the story of what
        happened next.
      </p>
      <h2>The email</h2>
      <p>
        Of all the categories, Inclusivity is the one I would have picked. I am
        hard of hearing, and Hearing Buddy started as the app I needed in rooms
        where I could not follow the conversation. As a disabled female founder,
        inclusivity and accessibility are the backbone of everything I do, and
        the brand is a colorful expression of my disability pride. To be
        recognized for that, specifically, meant more than the award itself.
      </p>
      <figure class="post-figure">
        <img
          src={`${base}/blog/top-linkedin-posts/02-ada-finalist.jpg`}
          alt="Apple Design Awards 2026 Finalist graphic for Hearing Buddy"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>Apple Design Awards 2026 Finalist, Inclusivity.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        It was also bittersweet. I went through my hearing loss journey with my
        real-life hearing buddy, Scott Krager. We built the app together, and
        now we were Finalists together. He is my biggest supporter in every part
        of my life, and the build that got us here was my best Liquid Glass work
        yet. I put my heart and soul into the interface. So we did what you do
        with news like that. We celebrated, and then we booked the trip. We were
        going to WWDC26.
      </p>
      <h2>Before Cupertino</h2>
      <p>
        The winners were announced a few days before the conference. Guitar Wiz
        took Inclusivity, and it earned it. I sent my congratulations and felt
        what I expected to feel, which was mostly gratitude. Finalist is a title
        you keep. The week in Cupertino was still ours.
      </p>
      <p>
        We landed and I did the only reasonable thing, which was take a Waymo to
        the hotel. The next morning I was up early for badge pickup and the
        WWDC26 swag, and then the week began.
      </p>
      <h2>The reception</h2>
      <p>
        The day before the keynote, Apple hosted a reception for every Finalist
        and winner. So much excellence in one photo. We got to showcase Hearing
        Buddy and talk to a bunch of people, and I loved sharing how Apple's
        latest technologies have changed my life in the most positive way, by
        helping me hear better in conversations.
      </p>
      <figure class="post-figure">
        <img
          src={`${base}/blog/wwdc26/finalists-group.jpg`}
          alt="The 2026 Apple Design Award Finalists and winners gathered around the WWDC26 sign at Apple Park"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          All of the Apple Design Award Finalists and winners at the WWDC26
          reception.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Hearing Buddy was up on the Inclusivity Finalists display, which I am
        not sure I will ever get used to. We found the Inclusivity winner and
        got a photo with them. Of course, I took a picture with the ADA award,
        like I do every time I go to WWDC. It is my dream to win one.
      </p>
      <figure class="video-figure small">
        <video
          controls
          playsinline
          preload="metadata"
          poster={`${base}/blog/wwdc26/inclusivity-display-poster.jpg`}
        >
          <source
            src={`${base}/blog/wwdc26/inclusivity-display.mp4`}
            type="video/mp4"
          />
        </video>
        <figcaption>
          Hearing Buddy on the Inclusivity Finalists display.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="post-figure">
        <img
          src={`${base}/blog/wwdc26/caption-with-pride.jpg`}
          alt="Lilly with the Inclusivity winner and Scott, all wearing caption with pride shirts"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          With the Inclusivity winner. We were Finalists in the same category.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Apple thought through every detail of the ADA experience, and I had so
        much fun that day with Scott.
      </p>
      <h2>The keynote</h2>
      <p>
        Then the keynote, and the State of the Union, where the Hearing Buddy
        logo showed up on screen. I still cannot believe that. Three
        announcements stood out. Apple Intelligence, SiriAI, and CoreAI, because
        we are following Apple's lead in the AI space and cannot wait to use
        these in Hearing Buddy. The child safety features, which would help us
        make a version of Hearing Buddy for kids. And the Liquid Glass
        improvements. We already had a Hearing Buddy build running on iOS 27,
        and it looked better with no coding needed.
      </p>
      <p>
        That last part is what I keep coming back to. Apple listened to
        developer feedback and made real improvements. We cannot wait to start
        coding iOS 27 features and dive even deeper into our Liquid Glass
        designs.
      </p>
      <h2>The Observatory</h2>
      <p>
        The peak of the week came last. We were invited to demo Hearing Buddy in
        the Observatory at Apple Park, and walking in for the first time was the
        most beautiful architecture I have ever experienced.
      </p>
      <figure class="post-figure">
        <img
          src={`${base}/blog/wwdc26/hello-selfie.jpg`}
          alt="Lilly and Scott in front of the hello sculpture inside the Observatory at Apple Park"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>Seeing the Observatory for the first time.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Inside, we showed Hearing Buddy to Apple and to the other Finalists, one
        conversation at a time. We also met the founding team of Tiimo, who won
        App of the Year in 2025. It was the best week ever.
      </p>
      <figure class="post-figure two">
        <div>
          <img
            src={`${base}/blog/wwdc26/showing-hearing-buddy.jpg`}
            alt="Lilly holding up Hearing Buddy on her iPhone in the Observatory"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <img
            src={`${base}/blog/wwdc26/developer-center-table.jpg`}
            alt="Lilly at a table with her phone between demos"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </div>
        <figcaption>Demoing Hearing Buddy in the Observatory.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <h2>Coming home</h2>
      <p>
        I built Hearing Buddy because I needed it. Standing in the Observatory
        as a Finalist, with Scott, showing the app to the people who make the
        platform, is a week I will carry for a long time. The dream is still to
        win one. We will be back.
      </p>

      <section class="post-outro reveal">
        <h2>Apple's 2026 Apple Design Awards</h2>
        <p>
          Hearing Buddy is listed as a Finalist in the Inclusivity category on
          <a href="https://developer.apple.com/design/awards/"
            >Apple's Design Awards page</a
          >, in
          <a
            href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/06/apple-reveals-winners-of-the-2026-apple-design-awards/"
            >Apple's newsroom announcement of the 2026 winners</a
          >, and in the App Store story
          <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/iphone/story/id1896567319"
            >Meet the 2026 Apple Design Award Finalists</a
          >. Apple's description: "Created by a solo developer with hearing
          loss, Hearing Buddy is shaped by experience. The thoughtful app uses
          the Foundation Models framework and on-device speech-to-text features
          to quickly generate real-time captions and summaries of spoken
          conversations."
        </p>
        <p>
          The posts this story comes from are on LinkedIn:
          <a
            href="https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7462194542281142272/"
            >the announcement</a
          >,
          <a
            href="https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7469837105968435201/"
            >the badge event</a
          >,
          <a
            href="https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7470243318669447168/"
            >the State of the Union</a
          >, and
          <a
            href="https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7470542551654195200/"
            >the Observatory</a
          >. Building something for the App Store and want it to stand out? <a
            href={`mailto:${profile.email}`}>Send me a note</a
          >.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</BlogLayout>
